"""Original heat, pollution and social-care access teaching."""
def course(c,m):
    return c("SWP-045","Heat Pollution and Environmental Health Inequity","heat-pollution-and-environmental-health-inequity","Heat Pollution and\nEnvironmental Health\nInequity",
        "Prepare a locally authorised heat-and-pollution support pathway that addresses access barriers, communicates uncertainty and stays within social work scope.",[
m("Exposure evidence and unequal access to protection","Distinguish heat and pollution information from an individual diagnosis and identify practical barriers to protection.",[
"Heat and air pollution can affect people in the same setting while requiring different information and response arrangements. A report that a room feels very hot, an official weather warning and a measured pollutant concentration describe different things. Ask what each source measures, where and when it applies and what authority issued it. A social worker should not infer an individual's diagnosis from an environmental reading. The purpose of this introductory assessment is to understand access and support needs and identify the appropriate source of technical or clinical advice. [S4, S5]",
"Exposure is shaped by time and place, while opportunities for protection can depend on housing, work, income, mobility, care responsibilities and available services. Ask what makes an official recommendation feasible or difficult for the person rather than assuming that knowledge alone changes the situation. A safe response may require coordination with a service controlling the relevant resource. The Port Harcourt contextual study supports attention to interacting conditions; it does not establish current temperatures, pollutant levels or the health status of every waterfront resident. Use actual local information for those questions. [R8, S1]",
"Check technical information carefully before interpreting a number. A pollution value requires the pollutant, unit, averaging period, location and relevant measurement quality. A general guideline overview is not an automatic Nigerian legal limit or a personal treatment threshold. A smell, visible haze or an unverified social-media message can justify recording a concern and seeking authorised assessment without establishing a measured concentration. Keep observation and inference separate. Do not claim that a single reading describes every indoor space or that the absence of an obvious smell proves safe air. [S5]",
"The 2026 African review maps pollution-related observational evidence with varied study designs and limited coverage. Its abstract reports that only three included studies considered temperature and none examined heat-respiratory interactions. Therefore, this course does not claim that the paper proves a combined heat-pollution effect or quantifies a local intervention benefit. The evidence supports asking careful exposure and access questions alongside official guidance. Clinical assessment, environmental sampling and regulatory determinations remain with the appropriately qualified and authorised roles. [R3, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a household reports a hot room and smoke nearby. A forwarded screenshot has no date or measurement unit. The worker records the concern, asks about access to approved support and checks official information. They do not treat the screenshot as a diagnosis or confirmed local concentration.",
"Prepare an evidence note separating reported experience, official advice and a technical measurement that still needs verification. Identify two access barriers and the appropriate advice needed.",
"A strong note preserves the concern without inventing a diagnosis or measured value. It records time, source and limits and avoids treating simultaneous exposures as proof of an interaction.",["S4","S5","R8","S1","R3"],[
("An environmental reading does not diagnose an individual.","A household reports heat and a worker sees an air-quality screenshot. Which conclusion stays within scope?","The information raises exposure and support questions requiring appropriate verification.",["The screenshot establishes the person's clinical diagnosis.","Every symptom has one proven environmental cause.","No authorised advice is needed if a number is shown."],"Environmental information and individual health assessment answer different questions."),
("Protection advice may be difficult to follow because of access barriers.","A person understands official advice but cannot reach the suggested service. What should the worker explore?","The practical access barrier and an appropriate authorised support route.",["A conclusion that the person lacks knowledge.","The assumption that understanding guarantees access.","A promise of a service place without checking availability."],"Information alone does not resolve resource or service constraints."),
("A pollutant number needs its measurement context.","A forwarded pollution value lacks a unit and averaging period. What should happen before interpretation?","Check the source, pollutant, unit, period, location and relevant measurement quality.",["Compare it with any number found online.","Describe it as a confirmed personal treatment threshold.","Assume it represents every nearby room."],"The meaning of a technical value depends on how and where it was measured."),
("Sensory observation is distinct from measured concentration.","Smoke is reported near a home. What is a defensible record?","A reported concern with the assessment or verification needed.",["A certified pollutant concentration based on smell.","A regulatory breach established by a generalist note alone.","Proof that air is safe whenever no smell is noticed."],"Observation can prompt action without becoming an unsupported technical finding."),
("The reviewed evidence does not establish heat-respiratory interactions.","What claim can be made from the 2026 African review used here?","It maps varied pollution evidence while leaving an interaction evidence gap.",["It proves the combined effect of heat and pollution for this household.","It tests the effectiveness of SWEEP's support pathway.","It supplies one validated local clinical threshold."],"The review's scope and limitations do not support those stronger claims."),
]),
m("Authorised messages and support conversations","Check the origin and relevance of a risk message and explain a feasible next support step without giving unqualified treatment advice.",[
"Use current, authorised public-health and environmental information when communicating about heat or pollution. Check the source, date, intended area, relevant period and any update or cancellation. A screenshot may have lost the context needed to interpret it. If information is uncertain, explain what is being checked and where a reliable update will come from. Do not create a warning threshold from an introductory lesson or describe a forwarded message as an official instruction simply because it appears to have a logo. [S4, S5]",
"Make the conversation accessible and practical. Ask how the person prefers to receive information and what the proposed next step would require. Use plain language and check understanding without testing or blaming them. Consider whether a contact method depends on data, electricity, literacy, language or a device that the person cannot reliably use. An alternative channel may be needed. Discuss relevant confidentiality and the scope of any agreed welfare contact before adding someone to a list or group. The worker's purpose is to support participation in an authorised pathway. [S1, S4]",
"Stay within role when a person asks about illness or medication. Refer clinical questions and urgent health concerns through the relevant authorised health or emergency route. Do not change a prescribed medicine, specify an individual fluid regimen or provide a treatment decision based on this course. A general public-health message does not replace personalised clinical advice. Similarly, advice about building alterations, ventilation equipment or protective technology may require qualified assessment of the setting. An apparently helpful change can conflict with outdoor pollution, security or another condition that must be considered. [S4, S5]",
"Agree a next step that can actually be followed. Check the receiving service's availability, access and role before promising support. State what the worker will do, what another role must decide and when the person can expect a follow-up. If a message changes, use the agreed channel to communicate the update and record the relevant contact through the approved process. The WHO executive summary organises heat-health action across sectors, but its European focus requires local adaptation. It does not establish a Nigerian referral protocol or prove that the proposed service is ready. [S4, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a person receives an old heat message and asks whether to change prescribed medicine. The worker checks the official current message, routes the medication question to an appropriate clinician and agrees an accessible contact method. Any urgent concern follows the relevant local response procedure.",
"Draft a short support conversation covering source/date checks, the person's access barrier, a scope-appropriate referral, understanding and follow-up. Do not include medication or individual treatment instructions.",
"An effective draft verifies the message, checks feasibility and names the next responsible role. It supports understanding while leaving clinical decisions with the appropriate professional.",["S4","S5","S1"],[
("An official-looking message still needs a date and source check.","A heat message has a familiar logo but no date or area. What should the worker do?","Verify its origin, current relevance and intended location before presenting it as guidance.",["Treat the logo as sufficient proof of a current warning.","Invent a validity period to make it usable.","Forward it as an instruction without checking context."],"Visual appearance does not establish current authority or applicability."),
("Communication channels must be usable by the intended person.","A welfare update is sent only through an app the person cannot access. What needs to change?","Agree a suitable accessible alternative through the authorised arrangement.",["Assume sending means the person received and understood it.","Blame the person for lacking a device.","Add their details to an unrelated public group."],"A message needs a usable route and proportionate information handling."),
("Medication questions require clinical advice.","A learner is asked whether a prescribed medicine should change during heat. What is appropriate?","Route the question to the relevant qualified clinician without altering treatment.",["Give an individual medication instruction from the course slides.","Assume every medicine should be stopped during hot weather.","Treat a public message as a personal prescription."],"The course does not qualify learners to make individual treatment decisions."),
("A practical change needs the relevant setting and expertise considered.","A suggested building change could conflict with outdoor pollution or security. What should the worker seek?","An appropriate qualified assessment and authorised local advice.",["A universal instruction copied without considering the setting.","A claim that any cooling-related change is automatically safe.","Untrained modification of equipment to test the idea."],"Interacting conditions can make a generic suggestion unsuitable."),
("A service's availability should be checked before support is promised.","A person needs an accessible receiving service. What should the next-step discussion clarify?","Availability, access, responsible roles and follow-up.",["A guaranteed place because the service appears in a directory.","A completed referral outcome as soon as a message is sent.","An admission decision made on another service's behalf."],"The agreed next step should reflect actual authority and capacity."),
]),
m("Service preparedness accountability and learning","Build a social-care readiness checklist and review whether authorised support reaches the people intended.",[
"A service preparedness plan connects information to responsible action. Identify who checks official updates, who can activate an agreed arrangement, which role contacts people and how a missed response is handled. Confirm staffing, communication, accessibility and the receiving service's role before describing readiness. A list of names or equipment is not a functioning pathway if nobody can use or maintain it. The WHO guidance emphasises coordinated planning; this teaching exercise applies that idea to a bounded social-care contribution rather than claiming to reproduce the complete heat-health plan. [S4, S1]",
"Review who may be missed by the arrangement. People without a stable contact channel, those working during service hours or those facing mobility barriers may need a suitable alternative. Ask about access rather than assuming an entire demographic group has one preference. Record only the information justified for the support purpose, protect access to welfare-contact details and follow the approved process when sharing with another role. A community contact can contribute local knowledge while remaining distinct from an authorised clinical assessment or an agency's protection responsibility. [S1, R8]",
"Test the process safely before relying on it. A tabletop exercise using fictional information can check whether a current message reaches the responsible role, whether an accessible contact works and whether the referral fallback is clear. Do not deliberately expose people to heat or pollution for a learning test. Record a missing step as a readiness issue with an owner and a review date. A successful simulation provides evidence about the tested process only; it does not prove that illness has been prevented or that every real-world condition has been covered. [S4, S1]",
"Monitoring should distinguish contacts attempted, contacts received, support accessed and the person's reported experience. A count of messages sent can conceal a failed channel. Investigate new barriers and use the authorised adjustment route. The pollution review's limited and mixed evidence does not validate this local pathway, while a descriptive contextual study cannot isolate its effect. Report the source, period and coverage of the monitoring information and avoid attributing health changes to the service without a suitable evaluation design and qualified input. Learning remains useful when its claims match its evidence. [R3, R8, S4]"],
"Fictional case: a tabletop exercise finds that the named contact role is unavailable and the fallback was never agreed. The service records the gap before claiming readiness. Later monitoring separates messages sent from people reached and support accessed, using no real personal details in the training exercise.",
"Create a readiness checklist with role, evidence, unresolved gap, owner and review date. Add three distinct pathway measures and one question about an excluded person's access.",
"A strong checklist tests a real responsibility and fallback, protects information and separates process evidence from health effects. A simulation or message count cannot certify effectiveness.",["S4","S1","R8","R3"],[
("Preparedness requires an operational role and fallback.","A contact list exists but its named role is unavailable. What does the service need before claiming readiness?","A confirmed responsible arrangement and an agreed fallback.",["Only a longer list with no role confirmation.","A success announcement because the document exists.","An assumption that any colleague will automatically respond."],"Readiness depends on the arrangement working, not only its documentation."),
("A safe tabletop exercise can use fictional information.","How can a learner test a communication pathway without exposing people to environmental hazards?","Use a supervised fictional scenario to check roles, access and fallback.",["Deliberately place participants in hazardous conditions.","Use identifiable records in an unapproved public chat.","Treat a live clinical emergency as a classroom exercise."],"Simulation can examine a process within appropriate safeguards and authority."),
("Simulation evidence is limited to the process tested.","A tabletop exercise succeeds. What can the team conclude?","The specified process worked in the exercise, with real-world effectiveness still unproven.",["Heat-related illness has been prevented in the population.","Every future service condition is covered.","No monitoring or update can be needed."],"A process test does not measure population health outcomes."),
("Sent messages and reached people are distinct measures.","A service records many messages but few confirmations of receipt. What should monitoring examine?","Whether the channel reaches people and what access barriers remain.",["The sent total as proof of successful support.","A health benefit inferred from the message volume.","A conclusion that all non-responders refuse help."],"The pathway has stages that need separate evidence."),
("A health-effect claim needs an appropriate evaluation design.","A service observes fewer reported health concerns during a later period. What should it avoid?","Claiming its pathway caused the change without suitable evidence and qualified input.",["Recording the period and coverage of reports.","Considering other environmental or service changes.","Seeking feedback about access to support."],"An observation over time does not isolate the pathway's effect."),
]),
])
